🔍 Explanation:
Java caches Integer values from -128 to 127.

When you write:


Integer i = 127;
Integer j = 127;
System.out.println(i == j);  // true ✅
Java reuses the same Integer object from its internal cache, so i == j is true.

But when you go beyond the cache range:


Integer i = 128;
Integer j = 128;
System.out.println(i == j);  // false ❌
Each 128 creates a new Integer object, so i == j compares references, not values — hence it's false.

✅ Correct Way to Compare Integer Values
Use .equals() instead of == for object comparison:


System.out.println(i.equals(j));  // true for 128
🔧 Why Java Does This
To save memory and improve performance, Java caches Integer values from -128 to 127 in the IntegerCache class.

